1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,091
Every monsoon, the street that floods is the street whose drain was reported weeks ago.

2
00:00:05,141 --> 00:00:06,382
The ticket said resolved.

3
00:00:06,432 --> 00:00:07,995
The drain was never cleared.

4
00:00:08,045 --> 00:00:10,577
We are Code Nirvana, and this is Nikasi:

5
00:00:10,627 --> 00:00:14,450
a drain ticket that will not close until a photo says so.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:22,450
A citizen photographs a blocked drain, and the phone adds the location.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:37,450
A vision model running in AWS Lambda describes the photo, Amazon Rekognition labels it, and the rain forecast for that exact spot sets the risk.

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:48,111
The ward officer opens the desk and sees every open drain ranked against the rain that is coming.

9
00:00:48,161 --> 00:00:54,685
Amazon Polly reads the morning brief aloud, in Hindi if you prefer.

10
00:00:54,735 --> 00:01:03,450
Each ticket shows what the model saw, who owns it, and how long before it escalates.

11
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:08,837
Nothing goes to a crew without a person.

12
00:01:08,887 --> 00:01:21,450
One click approves the dispatch, and the ticket's Step Functions workflow moves from waiting for the officer to waiting for the crew.

13
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:29,026
The crew clears the drain and takes a new photo.

14
00:01:29,076 --> 00:01:34,446
The ticket closes only if three checks pass:

15
00:01:34,496 --> 00:01:48,674
the drain reads as clear, the photo is within seventy-five metres of the spot, and it was taken after the dispatch.

16
00:01:48,724 --> 00:01:50,707
Three of three.

17
00:01:50,757 --> 00:01:51,385
Verified.

18
00:01:51,435 --> 00:01:55,450
Before, and after, the same drain.

19
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:01,017
If anyone misses a deadline, the ticket climbs:

20
00:02:01,067 --> 00:02:07,572
junior engineer, assistant, executive, ward commissioner, and every step is emailed.

21
00:02:07,622 --> 00:02:12,936
We shortened the clock so you can watch it.

22
00:02:12,986 --> 00:02:25,450
And if the new photo still shows the blockage, the fix is rejected and the ticket goes back, one level higher.

23
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:27,553
One honest note.

24
00:02:27,603 --> 00:02:34,505
Amazon Bedrock is blocked on our new account, and AWS support reviewed it and said no.

25
00:02:34,555 --> 00:02:39,719
So we ran an open-weight model inside Lambda, and we measured it.

26
00:02:39,769 --> 00:02:45,802
It scored every test photo a three, even a flooded street with no drain.

27
00:02:45,852 --> 00:02:51,450
So the model only writes a sentence, code decides, and a person approves.

28
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:55,130
Built on AWS by Code Nirvana.

29
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:59,450
The code and the live app are linked below.
